# File-context regexes for Tier-2.
#
# Unlike Tier-1, Tier-2 has NO host exec binary to label and NO
# file-context-driven type transition. The Tier-2 confinement domain
# (qdistro_tier2_t) is entered by podman, not by an execve of a labelled
# launcher:
#
#   - spawn-tier2.sh is a plain shell script (label_t / bin_t); it runs
#     in the caller's domain, NOT in qdistro_tier2_t.
#   - The CONTAINER PROCESS enters qdistro_tier2_t via podman's
#     `--security-opt label=type:qdistro_tier2_t` (a runtime process
#     label, equivalent to a SELinux exec-context override), not via a
#     domain_auto_trans keyed on a .fc-labelled binary.
#   - The per-container runtime dir ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/qdistro-tier2/<tok>)
#     and the sockets bound into it keep their HOST labels today
#     (user_tmp_t), because spawn-tier2.sh mounts them with plain
#     `-v ...:rw` and no `:z`/`:Z` relabel. Engaging qdistro_tier2_t
#     requires the launcher to add `:z`/`:Z` so the binds become
#     container_file_t (a launcher change, see README "Engaging the
#     domain"); a custom qdistro_tier2_file_t here would fight that
#     relabel and break the typebounds subset (container_t reaches its
#     volumes as container_file_t, not a qdistro type).
#
# This file is therefore intentionally (almost) empty — it exists so the
# module's three-file shape matches tier1 and so a future host-side
# Tier-2 helper that DOES need a deterministic label has an obvious home.
#
# If a future change adds a host-side Tier-2 exec helper under
# /usr/libexec/qdistro/, label it here, e.g.:
#   /usr/libexec/qdistro/qdistro-tier2-exec  --  gen_context(system_u:object_r:qdistro_tier2_exec_t,s0)
